# Line endings — every text file in this repository is LF, in the index AND in
# the working tree, on every platform.
#
# Why `eol=lf` and not merely `text=auto`: the whole toolchain writes LF, so a
# CRLF working tree fights it on every run, every commit and every pull.
#
#   * prettier's `endOfLine` defaults to `"lf"`. On a Windows checkout with
#     `core.autocrlf=true` (the git-for-Windows default) that made
#     `npm run lint` report **14,118 `Delete ␍` errors across 140 files** — and
#     `npm run format` "fixed" them by rewriting every file to LF, which git then
#     reported as modified with an EMPTY diff, aborting any pull that touched one
#     of them. Reproduced in a scratch clone of `main`; with `eol=lf` the same
#     clone lints clean and `npm run format` is a no-op.
#   * @tanstack/router-plugin regenerates `src/routeTree.gen.ts` with LF on every
#     dev/build — it compares the bytes it read from disk with the text it just
#     produced (`fs.writeFile`, LF on every OS). A CRLF checkout therefore made
#     the file permanently dirty and blocked pulls with:
#       error: Your local changes to the following files would be overwritten by
#       merge: src/routeTree.gen.ts
#   * vite/nitro output and node's `fs.writeFile` are LF-only as well.
#
# With `eol=lf`, what git checks out is exactly what every tool writes, so the
# working tree stays clean and formatting is idempotent.
#
# Cost: none for existing Linux/macOS checkouts (already LF), and none in the
# index — `git add --renormalize .` stages no changes (verified: 141 text files
# already LF; `System-v1.3.zip` and `public/favicon.ico` are detected binary,
# pinned `-text` below, and their blob hashes are unchanged).
#
# EXISTING WINDOWS CLONE — one time, after pulling this file, with a clean
# `git status` (it re-materialises the working tree as LF):
#
#   git rm --cached -r .
#   git reset --hard
#
# Then `git ls-files --eol` shows `w/lf` for every text file, `npm run lint`
# goes green, and `npm run format` stops dirtying the tree.
* text=auto eol=lf

# `src/routeTree.gen.ts` is generated, so discarding a local copy is always safe
# (`git checkout -- src/routeTree.gen.ts`, or any dev/build run, reproduces the
# committed bytes). Pinned explicitly as well as by the rule above so the reason
# stays visible next to the file it concerns — see `src/routes/README.md`.
src/routeTree.gen.ts text eol=lf

# Binary — never convert. Git auto-detects both already; stated so the intent is
# explicit and a future `.zip`/icon cannot be normalised by accident.
*.zip -text
*.ico -text
